# Day 9 - GitHub & Pull Request Workflow

## Commands & Actions Learned

git clone <repo_url>
→ Clone repository from GitHub

git push
→ Upload changes to GitHub

git pull
→ Fetch and update latest changes

git checkout -b <branch_name>
→ Create new branch for feature work

git merge <branch_name>
→ Merge branch into main

## Key Concepts

GitHub = Cloud platform to store and manage Git repositories

Remote Repository = Code stored on GitHub

Local Repository = Code on your system

Pull Request (PR) = Request to merge code changes into main branch

## Workflow (Real World)

1. Clone repository
2. Create new branch
3. Make changes
4. Commit changes
5. Push branch to GitHub
6. Create Pull Request
7. Review & merge into main

## Pull Request (PR) Flow

Feature Branch → Push to GitHub
↓
Create Pull Request
↓
Code Review
↓
Merge into Main Branch

## Example Flow

git checkout -b feature1
(make changes)

git add .
git commit -m "feature added"

git push origin feature1

→ Create PR on GitHub
→ Merge into main

## Observations

* Code is not directly pushed to main branch
* PR ensures review before merging
* Multiple developers can work safely
* GitHub maintains version history

## Debugging Learned

* If push fails → check remote connection
* If branch not visible → push branch first
* If conflicts occur → resolve manually before merge
* Always pull latest changes before starting work

## What I Understood

* GitHub enables collaboration
* Pull Requests are essential for team workflow
* Branching + PR = safe development
* Code review improves quality

## Real Life Use

* Used in all software companies
* Teams collaborate using PRs
* CI/CD pipelines trigger on PR or push
* Essential for DevOps and development workflows
